thought to on now so Patrick valance the  UK government's chief scientific adviser  a very good morning to you look first  and foremost what is your assessment of  where we stand this morning well let me  start by just saying I offer my sincere  condolences to the relatives and friends  of the unfortunate patient who died  where are we now we've got more cases in  the UK we expect to get more this is the  start of an outbreak clearly we have  cases across Europe across the world  this is a global epidemic and we would  expect to see more cases in the UK what  does that mean I mean what sort of  numbers are there must be there must be  analysis projections that have been put  into place by the government on this  what sort of figures are we looking at  yes so we've been looking at this right  from the outset we've had mathematical  modelers experts in epidemiology looking  at it and we've got a reasonable  worst-case scenario which is which is  looking at the the situation that could  evolve in the worst worst outcome  reasonable worst outcome and that  involves large numbers of people 80% of  the population getting some sort of  infection and as we've said we think the  mortality rate is 1% or lower of course  that's the reason worst case now there's  a planning situation I expect it to be  less than that and so does the chief  medical officer and the reasons for that  is if you look at what's happened in  Wuhan it's not 80 percent of the  population that's been infected it's a  much lower number and we think that the  mortality rate as I say is under 1% so  we do expect significant increases we  can't predict exactly how this will go  in the UK and we're doing everything  that we need to at the moment to try to  contain and are preparing to move to the  delay phase and the delay phase is  important because what that means is  trying to suppress the peak so we  haven't got so many patients getting it  all of one time and if possible move it  a bit so that we move more into the  summer months where the NHS is obviously  better able to cope and other  respiratory viruses circulating tend to  be a bit less in terms of this four  stage play  it was only announced on Monday control  seems to have disappeared relatively  quickly was this not introduced quickly  enough oh I think no we've been very  careful on timing and contain is still  where we are we are looking cases or  isolated there's contact tracing going  on and of course these aren't absolutely  discrete phases that go from ones or the  other abruptly you merge into them and  preparation for delay is well underway  and the key thing is that we need to  take the actions to protect ourselves  and make sure that we take action to  look after everybody and particularly  the vulnerable you talk about moving  into delay phase what does that actually  n tale for me not only governments for  institutions but what do we need to do  as individuals well as individuals the  advice is very clear and it's simple and  it's about looking after yourself and  making sure that we do things that also  protect everybody and that's hand  washing very very important very simple  and also for those who have got the  right travel connections and have got  symptoms it's about rapid staying at  home isolating yourself at home and  making sure that you call one-one-one  and get into the right system there  those are the simple things that can be  done when we move to another situation  as other countries are now doing in  terms of delay the things that you might  look at and it's in the plan that's been  published include measures such as  getting a wider diagnostic criteria to  ask more people to stay at home if they  get symptoms to looking at things like  school closures mass gatherings and so  on but there are other things you do  first and the key thing is during any  progression to think about protecting  the vulnerable  first and foremost so there are some  simple things that can be done there